RESTAPI
*A REST API (Representational State Transfer API) enables communication between client and server over HTTP. It exchanges data typically in JSON format using standard web protocols.
*Uses HTTP methods like GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, and DELETE.
Client sends requests to server endpoints (URLs).
Server returns responses such as JSON, XML, HTML, or images.
Maps HTTP methods to CRUD operations (Create, Read, Update, Delete).
*In REST architecture, the main HTTP methods are GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, and DELETE, which map to CRUD operations. Other less commonly used methods include HEAD and OPTIONS.

FASTAPI
*FastAPI is a modern Python web framework, very efficient in building APIs. It is based on Pythons type hints feature that has been added since Python 3.6 onwards.
*To install FastAPI (preferably in a virtual environment), use pip3 installer.
  pip3 install fastapi
*FastAPI doesnt come with any built-in server application. To run FastAPI app, you need an ASGI(Asynchronous Server Gateway Interface) server called uvicorn
 pip3 install uvicorn

*hello world program
*The first step in creating a FastAPI app is to declare the application object of FastAPI class.
*This app object is the main point of interaction of the application with the client browser. 
*The async keyword in the functions definition tells FastAPI that it is to be run asynchronously
*create a venv and create new folder(hello.py) and insert this code
*To run the server -> uvicorn hello:app --reload
from fastapi import FastAPI
app=FastAPI()

@app.get("/")
async def hello():
    return {"hello":"world"}

